y video than\\n0:49 - usual but the results are really\\n0:51 - interesting I respect your time so we\'ll\\n0:53 - get straight into the inspiration for\\n0:55 - this and how it works Back in the early\\n\\n[DIMPLE INSPIRATION - 0:58]\\n0:58 - 1900s like many other golfers William\\n1:01 - Taylor wondered why damaged golf balls\\n1:03 - performed better than new smooth ones An\\n1:07 - optician by profession he made a DIY\\n1:10 - wind tunnel to study the vortices and\\n1:12 - eddies in smoke swirling around\\n1:15 - potential new surfaces He saw that\\n1:17 - evenly spaced indentations created the\\n1:21 - best air flow And by the 1930s it was\\n1:24 - hard to find golf balls without them as\\n1:26 - they were so good at long and stable\\n1:29 - flight With these results it\'s easy to\\n1:32 - see why this breakthrough has been on\\n1:33 - the mind of engineers all over the world\\n1:36 - All of them trying to reduce the drag\\n1:39 - and improve the efficiency of cars boats\\n1:42 - turbines and propellers Some of you may\\n1:45 - already know how the dimples on a golf\\n1:47 - ball works but it\'s useful to have a\\n1:48 - quick refresher as it helps explain some\\n1:50 - of the more recent breakthroughs When a\\n\\n[ENGINEERING OF A GOLF BALL - 1:51]\\n1:52 - golf ball flies through the air it has\\n1:54 - to push air out of the way Doing this\\n1:57 - creates drag which is the force that\\n1:59 - slows it down If the ball was smooth the\\n2:02 - air would flow around it in a way that\\n2:04 - leaves a big low pressure wake behind it\\n2:08 - This is because the air becomes detached\\n2:10 - from the ball That low pressure wake\\n2:13 - pulls back on the ball as if it was\\n2:15 - being sucked backwards and creates a lot\\n2:18 - of drag But dimples change how the air\\n2:22 - flows They create a thin layer of\\n2:24 - swirling air called turbulent flow right\\n2:27 - next to the ball\'s surface energizing\\n2:31 - the boundary layer Because this\\n2:33 - turbulent boundary layer has more energy\\n2:36 - it can resist detaching from the ball\'s\\n2:39 - surface This causes the air to stick to\\n2:41 - the ball longer and reduces the size of\\n2:44 - the wake behind it A smaller wake means\\n2:47 - less suction force pulling back the ball\\n2:50 - and aka less drag In more technical\\n2:53 - terms the dimples are helping to reduce\\n2:56 - the pressure drag That drag caused by\\n2:58 - the low pressure zone at the back of the\\n3:00 - golf ball But what we can\'t forget here\\n3:02 - is that we also have skin friction drag\\n3:06 - I always found this type of drag more\\n3:08 - intuitive as it\'s like the friction\\n3:10 - slowing down a skidding object except\\n3:13 - the friction is with the air instead of\\n3:15 - the floor The dimples actually increase\\n3:18 - the skin friction drag because the\\n3:20 - turbulent air clings more tightly to the\\n3:23 - ball However this trade-off between\\n3:25 - friction and pressure drag is worth it\\n3:28 - for a golf ball These findings are all\\n3:30 - well and good for the golf course but\\n3:32 - there are two main differences that\\n3:34 - change how these can be applied to other\\n3:36 - objects and these are the shape and\\n3:39 - movement Clearly the shape of a ball and\\n3:42 - an aeraf foil or propeller blade is very\\n3:45 - different This causes a potential\\n3:47 - problem because it\'s the blunt shape of\\n3:50 - a ball that makes the dimples work so\\n3:53 - well If it was streamlined like an aeraf\\n3:55 - foil there wouldn\'t be the huge low\\n3:57 - pressure wake to get rid of Secondly the\\n4:01 - way a golf ball moves through the air is\\n4:03 - quite unique Its dimples not only reduce\\n4:06 - drag but also enhance the Magnus effect\\n4:09 - a phenomenon that generates lift when\\n4:11 - the ball is spinning due to the\\n4:13 - deflection of air There are wings that\\n4:16 - use the Magnus effect like this one from\\n4:18 - the awesome Project Air YouTube channe